WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.992
This is payments lecture 3. What the request carries, and in what format.

2
00:00:05.342 --> 00:00:13.321
In lecture 1 you tapped a card for ₹250. The terminal asked: may I charge 250?

3
00:00:13.671 --> 00:00:17.670
Today we open that question and read it, byte by byte.

4
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.953
Do I need anything before this?

5
00:00:20.303 --> 00:00:26.189
Only bits, bytes and hex, from the bits and bytes note. 8 switches make a byte.

6
00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:28.945
Each half of a byte is one hex digit.

7
00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:31.456
First, where the message goes.

8
00:00:31.806 --> 00:00:36.414
The terminal sends the request to the shop's bank. That is the acquirer.

9
00:00:36.764 --> 00:00:42.695
The shop's bank sends it to the card network. Visa, Mastercard, or RuPay.

10
00:00:43.045 --> 00:00:48.135
The network looks at the first digits of the card number, and sends it to the

11
00:00:48.135 --> 00:00:50.250
card's bank. That is the issuer.

12
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:55.656
The card's bank says yes or no, and the answer travels back the same way.

13
00:00:56.006 --> 00:01:02.794
Between the banks and the network, these two messages are written in one format:

14
00:01:02.794 --> 00:01:03.558
ISO 8583.

15
00:01:03.908 --> 00:01:06.445
Why not just send text, like a modern app?

16
00:01:06.795 --> 00:01:13.375
Because it is old. The version most networks build on is from 1987, when every

17
00:01:13.375 --> 00:01:14.725
byte cost money.

18
00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:21.243
So there are no labels. A modern system writes "amount": 250.00. This format

19
00:01:21.243 --> 00:01:25.544
just sends 12 digits, at a place both sides agree on.

20
00:01:25.894 --> 00:01:30.308
Short and fast. But you cannot read it without the rule book.

21
00:01:30.658 --> 00:01:34.316
Every message has three parts, always in this order.

22
00:01:34.666 --> 00:01:37.499
One. The message type. Four digits.

23
00:01:37.849 --> 00:01:43.081
Two. The bitmap. 64 switches that say which fields are present.

24
00:01:43.431 --> 00:01:48.479
Three. The fields. Only the ones the bitmap switched on, in number order.

25
00:01:48.829 --> 00:01:53.992
Part one. The message type of our request is 0100.

26
00:01:54.342 --> 00:01:56.689
Read it one digit at a time.

27
00:01:57.039 --> 00:02:02.256
The first digit, 0, is the version: the 1987 standard.

28
00:02:02.606 --> 00:02:07.104
The second digit, 1, is the class: an authorisation. May I?

29
00:02:07.454 --> 00:02:12.732
The third digit, 0, is the function: a question. 1 would mean an answer.

30
00:02:13.082 --> 00:02:17.263
The fourth digit, 0, says who started it: the shop's side.

31
00:02:17.613 --> 00:02:26.447
Your turn. What is 0110? And 0400, if class 4 means reversal? Pause now.

32
00:02:28.947 --> 00:02:35.005
0110. Class 1, function 1. The answer to an authorisation.

33
00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:42.647
0400. Class 4, function 0. A reversal question: cancel that one.

34
00:02:42.997 --> 00:02:44.735
Part two. The bitmap.

35
00:02:45.085 --> 00:02:51.237
There are 128 possible fields, but a payment needs only 10 to 20.

36
00:02:51.587 --> 00:02:57.999
So instead of labelling each field, the message starts with 64 switches. Switch

37
00:02:57.999 --> 00:03:00.191
2 on means field 2 is here.

38
00:03:00.541 --> 00:03:07.752
8 switches are 1 byte. So 64 switches are 8 bytes. In hex, 16 characters.

39
00:03:08.102 --> 00:03:14.502
Here is the first row of 8. In our message, switches 2, 3, 4 and 7 are on.

40
00:03:14.852 --> 00:03:17.821
This is the part I asked about. How are they numbered?

41
00:03:18.171 --> 00:03:23.893
Every switch has two labels. Look at the top: its number. Counted from the left,

42
00:03:23.893 --> 00:03:27.255
starting at 1. That number is the field number.

43
00:03:27.605 --> 00:03:34.503
Look at the bottom: its worth. 8, 4, 2, 1, and again 8, 4, 2, 1. The worth is

44
00:03:34.503 --> 00:03:36.922
only used to write the hex.

45
00:03:37.272 --> 00:03:46.386
Write one bit per switch, left to right. Switch 1 off: 0. Switches 2, 3, 4 on: 1

46
00:03:46.386 --> 00:03:49.804
1 1. 5 and 6 off. 7 on. 8 off.

47
00:03:50.154 --> 00:03:54.847
So the pattern is 0111 0010.

48
00:03:55.197 --> 00:04:02.947
Left half: 0×8 + 1×4 + 1×2 + 1×1.

49
00:04:03.297 --> 00:04:08.963
= 0 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 7. Hex digit 7.

50
00:04:09.313 --> 00:04:17.117
Right half: 0×8 + 0×4 + 1×2 + 0×1 = 2. Hex digit 2.

51
00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:22.448
Side by side, the byte is hex 72.

52
00:04:22.798 --> 00:04:27.875
In the bits note I counted the worth from the right. One, two, four, eight. That

53
00:04:27.875 --> 00:04:29.462
is why it felt backwards.

54
00:04:29.812 --> 00:04:35.415
Exactly. Both are true at once. The worth grows from the right. The switch

55
00:04:35.415 --> 00:04:39.730
number grows from the left. Switch 1 is the leftmost one.

56
00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:49.841
The standard reads it the same way. Hex 82 is 1000 0010. That means fields 1 and

57
00:04:49.841 --> 00:04:50.085
7.

58
00:04:50.435 --> 00:04:57.333
Your turn. Row 2 holds fields 9 to 16. In our message, fields 11 and 14 are on.

59
00:04:57.333 --> 00:05:01.350
Write the pattern and the hex byte. Pause now.

60
00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:09.506
Field 11 is the 3rd switch of the row. Field 14 is the 6th.

61
00:05:09.856 --> 00:05:14.656
Pattern: 0010 0100.

62
00:05:15.006 --> 00:05:22.767
Left half: only the 2 is on: 2. Right half: only the 4 is on: 4. The byte is 24.

63
00:05:23.117 --> 00:05:27.234
Do the same for all 8 rows, and you get the whole bitmap.

64
00:05:27.584 --> 00:05:35.037
72 24 04 80 08 E0 82 00

65
00:05:35.387 --> 00:05:42.757
And notice: switch 1 is off. Switch 1 on would mean a second bitmap follows, for

66
00:05:42.757 --> 00:05:46.442
fields 65 to 128. We need none of those.

67
00:05:46.792 --> 00:05:51.548
Part three. The fields. Here are the important ones in our request.

68
00:05:51.898 --> 00:05:58.199
Field 2, the card number. Field 3, what kind of transaction: all zeros means a

69
00:05:58.199 --> 00:06:00.622
purchase. Field 4, the amount.

70
00:06:00.972 --> 00:06:07.397
Field 11, the trace number. Field 22, how the card was read: 07 means

71
00:06:07.397 --> 00:06:08.980
contactless chip.

72
00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:16.508
Field 37, a reference that stays with this payment through refunds and disputes.

73
00:06:16.508 --> 00:06:21.353
41, the terminal. 49, the currency. 55, the chip data.

74
00:06:21.703 --> 00:06:26.383
If there are no labels and no spaces, how does the reader know where one field

75
00:06:26.383 --> 00:06:26.683
ends?

76
00:06:27.033 --> 00:06:33.681
Two ways. Most fields are fixed. The rule book says field 4 is always 12 digits.

77
00:06:33.681 --> 00:06:35.676
So the reader counts 12.

78
00:06:36.026 --> 00:06:42.534
Some fields change length, like the card number. Those start with their length.

79
00:06:42.534 --> 00:06:47.559
Field 2 starts with 16, then 16 digits. That is called LLVAR.

80
00:06:47.909 --> 00:06:54.073
A longer field, like the chip data, uses three digits of length: LLLVAR.

81
00:06:54.423 --> 00:06:57.772
Now the amount. It has no decimal point.

82
00:06:58.122 --> 00:07:04.693
Field 4 is 12 digits: 000000025000.

83
00:07:05.043 --> 00:07:08.584
Drop the zeros in front: 25000.

84
00:07:08.934 --> 00:07:16.149
Field 49 says 356. That is the rupee, which has 2 digits after the point. So the

85
00:07:16.149 --> 00:07:17.862
number is in paise.

86
00:07:18.212 --> 00:07:24.442
25000 paise ÷ 100 = ₹250.00

87
00:07:24.792 --> 00:07:29.127
Whole numbers only. Nothing in this message is ever a double.

88
00:07:29.477 --> 00:07:39.824
Your turn. Field 4 is 000000150075. Field 49 is 356. How much is the payment?

89
00:07:39.824 --> 00:07:41.168
Pause now.

90
00:07:44.168 --> 00:07:52.273
150075 paise. ÷ 100 = 1500, remainder 75.

91
00:07:52.623 --> 00:07:56.843
= ₹1,500.75

92
00:07:57.193 --> 00:08:00.862
Now the answer: message 0110.

93
00:08:01.212 --> 00:08:07.339
The card's bank copies the fields that identify the question. The most important

94
00:08:07.339 --> 00:08:09.637
is field 11, the trace number.

95
00:08:09.987 --> 00:08:12.157
Why is that the most important one?

96
00:08:12.507 --> 00:08:17.831
Because the shop's bank sends thousands of questions, and the answers come back

97
00:08:17.831 --> 00:08:22.819
in any order. The trace number says which question each answer belongs to.

98
00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:29.381
The card's bank also adds two fields of its own. Field 38, the approval code,

99
00:08:29.381 --> 00:08:33.980
printed on your receipt. And field 39, the response code.

100
00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:36.613
00 means approved.

101
00:08:36.963 --> 00:08:43.968
05: do not honour, a no without a reason. 14: no such card number. 51: not

102
00:08:43.968 --> 00:08:45.199
enough money.

103
00:08:45.549 --> 00:08:53.049
54: the card has expired. 55: wrong PIN. 91: the card's bank could not be

104
00:08:53.049 --> 00:08:53.871
reached.

105
00:08:54.221 --> 00:08:59.654
When a terminal says declined, it is one of these two characters, turned into

106
00:08:59.654 --> 00:09:00.077
words.

107
00:09:00.427 --> 00:09:03.115
You will meet a few other message types.

108
00:09:03.465 --> 00:09:09.331
0100 is an authorisation: may I? The money does not move yet.

109
00:09:09.681 --> 00:09:16.769
0200 is a financial message: the money is meant to move. ATMs use these.

110
00:09:17.119 --> 00:09:23.721
0400 is a reversal: cancel that one. For example, when the answer never arrived

111
00:09:23.721 --> 00:09:25.810
and the terminal gave up.

112
00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:32.065
0800 is a network check: are you still there? No customer involved.

113
00:09:32.415 --> 00:09:34.669
So when does the money actually move?

114
00:09:35.019 --> 00:09:39.866
Later, in end-of-day files between the banks. That is lecture 7.

115
00:09:40.216 --> 00:09:42.733
Three things are not in the message.

116
00:09:43.083 --> 00:09:49.986
The PIN in plain digits. When a PIN is needed, it travels encrypted, in a field

117
00:09:49.986 --> 00:09:51.908
of its own. Lecture 6.

118
00:09:52.258 --> 00:09:58.120
The CVV on the back of the card. A chip payment does not use it. It is for

119
00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:00.814
typing card details into websites.

120
00:10:01.164 --> 00:10:04.748
And your name. The bank does not need it to decide.

121
00:10:05.098 --> 00:10:11.321
What makes the payment hard to fake is field 55: the chip's one-time code, the

122
00:10:11.321 --> 00:10:13.635
cryptogram. Lectures 4 and 5.

123
00:10:13.985 --> 00:10:16.033
Let's remember five lines.

124
00:10:16.383 --> 00:10:19.694
A message is a type, a bitmap, and fields.

125
00:10:20.044 --> 00:10:23.884
The switch number is the field number, counted from the left.

126
00:10:24.234 --> 00:10:29.478
Each half of 4 switches is one hex digit, worth 8, 4, 2, 1.

127
00:10:29.828 --> 00:10:35.503
The amount is whole paise, 12 digits, with the currency in field 49.

128
00:10:35.853 --> 00:10:43.721
The answer 0110 copies field 11, and field 39 = 00 means yes.

129
00:10:44.071 --> 00:10:49.664
Last, six interview questions you can now answer. Try each one out loud.

130
00:10:50.014 --> 00:10:56.404
Walk me through a card authorisation. What is an MTI, and what does 0110 mean?

131
00:10:56.404 --> 00:11:00.664
How does the receiver know which fields are present?

132
00:11:01.014 --> 00:11:07.888
Why are amounts sent in minor units? How is an answer matched to its question?

133
00:11:07.888 --> 00:11:10.973
And when is a reversal (0400) sent?

134
00:11:11.323 --> 00:11:16.790
Next is lecture 4: the tap, and what the card and the terminal say to each

135
00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:17.233
other.
